━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
+ LazyPolicy is U8

` Where a lazy local takes its value, as the reference's planner settles it from the uses.
` - `lazy-inline`: its one read evaluates the value, each time the read runs. One declared in
`   a loop is not read in a loop of its turn or in a nested function.
` - `lazy-first-read`: a local declared in a loop and read more than once is evaluated by the
`   first of its reads, each time that read runs, and the others read what it stored.
` - `lazy-memo`: the value is evaluated once in a run of the function, by whichever read
`   comes first, which a flag cleared where the function starts remembers. An assignment
`   stores its value and sets the flag, so the value declared is not evaluated after it.
`   One declared in a loop is evaluated once a turn: its flag is cleared again where the
`   block that declares it starts, as TypeScript clears it, since a read may come before
`   the line that declares it. A local read tracked, one whose value reads something tracked
`   outside a loop, and one referred to, whose address a read of the local evaluates and
`   takes, are kept so too, as the reference keeps them behind a flag.
` - `lazy-shared`: nested functions capture the local. Its value and its flag live in the
`   environment of the function that declares it, and whichever function reads it first
`   evaluates it there, once for the environment. An assignment stores and sets the flag.
`   Where the program's tables find that the function that declares it can give it its value
`   before it makes each nested function that reads it, the environment holds the value alone,
`   as an eager local's, and that function keeps the flag (`holds-lazy`).
` - `lazy-unsettled`: no rule here yet: one read in a state machine or in a loop on a stream
`   that no nested function captures it for, or one captured that is declared with others or
`   referred to.
` The locals one declaration names together take their values together, once in a run of
` the function, by whichever read of any of them comes first: `lazy-memo` for each, or
` unsettled where one of them is assigned or would be unsettled alone.
` A local that gives a property its value is evaluated for the property apart from this, and
` its reads in the values of other such locals are settled as any local's. A constructor that
` builds its own class reads the property itself where its body names one.

| lazy-unsettled = 0
| lazy-inline = 1
| lazy-first-read = 2
| lazy-memo = 3
| lazy-shared = 4

